d/id #uuid "e2ce7fcb-cc48-5f67-a313-8284ed75ba4a" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-da239506-5511-599b-8ddb-c93e5896ff1d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da239506-5511-599b-8ddb-c93e5896ff1d" "answer-e151374e-b181-5980-8595-359e1cd1cb3f" "answer-4bf70411-1f19-53a2-a3b8-9a1b12b5ec70" "answer-f00b04be-c45d-5315-b8e3-7fe8b852f7a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da239506-5511-599b-8ddb-c93e5896ff1d" :answer/id #uuid "da239506-5511-599b-8ddb-c93e5896ff1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e151374e-b181-5980-8595-359e1cd1cb3f" :answer/id #uuid "e151374e-b181-5980-8595-359e1cd1cb3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bf70411-1f19-53a2-a3b8-9a1b12b5ec70" :answer/id #uuid "4bf70411-1f19-53a2-a3b8-9a1b12b5ec70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f00b04be-c45d-5315-b8e3-7fe8b852f7a9" :answer/id #uuid "f00b04be-c45d-5315-b8e3-7fe8b852f7a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c1d729e8-d1cb-584b-b25e-2da8d463c255" :question/id #uuid "c1d729e8-d1cb-584b-b25e-2da8d463c255" :question/math-academy-id "q-363831" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a61a0e53-ff3b-5bd0-a596-7b55277e90f4" "answer-field-3b135284-152c-5479-bf6a-2432206a29ed" "answer-field-bde64c22-aceb-5850-9ac5-72a00519f50e" "answer-field-01393794-e39b-5872-af64-efa4c600b9fc" "answer-field-d28e5083-6111-5659-843b-b5cd8118683f" "answer-field-2d46592f-4a1b-598c-8e05-b401c0a291b3" "answer-field-bdb7b068-f570-5e7a-8102-dc37c8fd15fd" "answer-field-919a4379-648e-5105-89b6-5c1b6bf4b216" "answer-field-cd7c56fa-7567-5c85-b0fb-c825f694718e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a61a0e53-ff3b-5bd0-a596-7b55277e90f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "a61a0e53-ff3b-5bd0-a596-7b55277e90f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-32c29a9e-feec-5baf-8d10-2cf59a182365" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dbfbf452-d9f3-5685-a5ea-cc7fef34b55d" "answer-55b63e04-